{
  "dom_id": "step-q15687",
  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14275039/assets/339662001ca92041da1f2d3531c3e841e987a7eff256d5ead1d23255ccee238a.png)\n\nFind the $x$-intercepts of the circle $(x-2)^{2}+(y-2)^{2}=20,$ shown above.",
  "worked_solution": "We must have $y=0$ at the $x$-intercepts. \n\nSo, we need to substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the circle and solve for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2}+(y-2)^{2} & =20 \\\\ (x-2)^{2}+(0-2)^{2} & =20 \\\\ (x-2)^{2}+4 & =20 \\\\ (x-2)^{2} & =16 \\\\ x-2 & =\\pm \\sqrt{16} \\\\ x & =2\\pm 4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $x$-intercepts occur at the points $(-2,0)$ and $(6,0).$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
